[Music]  our next speaker  is associate professor james schroer  from australia  he's the head of epidemiological  modelling unit at school of public  health at monash university  over to you james my name is james  trower  and i'm a respiratory and public health  physician  and i'm also head of epidemiological  modelling at the school of public health  and preventive medicine at monash  university  and i'm going to be talking about real  time modeling of a pandemic  and the challenges that has thrown up  this year clearly the pandemics had a  huge  impact on everybody's lives and the  economy and society as well as health  and it's been a huge challenge for  modelers as well it's been something  totally unprecedented from my point of  view  i've previously been a modeler that's  focused on tuberculosis being a  respiratory infection  and um it's a very different situation  the challenges of modeling a pandemic  mean that the ground is always  moving under you the moment you try to  simulate something the epidemic has  changed  and you're always chasing your tail  trying to understand what the data means  and by the time you've produced a  simulation uh it's often  out of date or the model needs to be  recalibrated the following week  so i'm going to talk about some of the  issues around  covert 19 and understanding its  epidemiology and transmission and how  we've tried to put that into models  and what that can tell us about the  future pathway of the epidemic  so to start off  i would like to introduce probably the  most widely used framework for  infectious disease modeling this is an  seir model um there's a lot a lot of  different things that can be referred to  as a model but when people talk about  mathematical modeling we're usually  talking about some sort of  mechanistic framework and i like the  word mechanistic because it  it conveys the fact that we're trying to  explicitly represent  the mechanism of disease transmission  that underlies what's happening in the  population  and so the seir framework represents um  the population as being in these  compartments  so each compartment is associated with  the number which is the number of people  in that state in relation to covert 19  and so the s compartment is the number  of people susceptible  the e compartment is the number of  people who have been exposed but haven't  yet developed symptoms  the eye compartment is the number of  people who are now symptomatic and also  infectious  and then as they transition into the r  compartment they recover or become  removed from the system and in this  framework they can't then be  infected again and so it's a pretty  simple system it's only  comprised of four compartments um and  if you think about the r naught of the  epidemic that's the number of people who  are infected by one infective person on  average in a fully susceptible  population  and this model does a pretty good job of  capturing some of the dynamics of that  early phase  of epidemic growth so in the  illustration on the left here you can  see that we have one person  infecting two people in the next  generation of infection infections  and then that progresses to four eight  sixteen and the model  if you just set it up as described in  that seir  framework uh you get that exponential  growth and the model does a pretty good  job of capturing that  uh you also get the general bell-shaped  curve of the epidemic and we've all seen  a lot of curves over recent years  sorry over recent months and  we've probably got a little bit bored of  looking at curves and being told to  flatten the curve  and and the curve itself if we think  about an epic  unmitigated epidemic and an epidemic in  which  nothing is done to control the virus  does look indeed a bit like a bell shape  that's what  we would predict from the models that's  what the sci framework can show us  and in reality if we had it done nothing  to control the epidemic we probably  would have got this very large bell  shape  so the model tells us a little bit about  that it tells us that the peak of the  bell is the point at which herd immunity  is reached and the downslope  after that peak is actually extra  infections that are not required to  reach herd immunity so we know that if  we were to let the epidemic play out  totally unmitigated we would actually  have far more infections than we would  need to prevent a  second wave and so people would not  necessarily  be infected and die and we it also tells  us that as we get towards  the tail that tail off  occurs because the number of  susceptibles in the population gradually  decreases it doesn't drop down to zero  but it gradually decreases  so this is the sort of framework that  was used prior to the pandemic by  organizations like the cdc to propose a  response but it was very much informed  by  the expected response that we would need  to have to influenza  given that so many new pandemics are  caused by influenza viruses so the plan  really for uh for an influenza pandemic  and responding to it was to mitigate the  epidemic just to flatten the curve  so uh this is certainly what flattening  the curve meant to me in march it seems  to mean something totally different now  but in in sort of the early months of  the year flattening the curve in  australia meant that we were  anticipating a very large bell-shaped  epidemic and we were going to try to  squash that down to a much  much flatter bell but still a pretty  substantial  epidemic we were never at that stage  trying  to totally eliminate or suppress the  epidemic  the idea was that if we could flatten  the curve to some extent we could keep  the number of  cases below what was what would have  overwhelmed our hospital capacity but  still allows substantial community  transmission  and that is essentially what happened in  the 2009 influenza pandemic we didn't  totally stamp it out probably somewhere  around 15  or a little under if the population were  infected by 2009 h1n1  influenza and uh  that may still happen i suppose in the  case of covert 19 but at this stage it  seems like a lot of countries are really  pushing towards uh well countries like  australia and new zealand are certainly  pushing towards having very low levels  of transmission and trying to keep  things such that a very small proportion  of the population  so in these early months of the year  models were  already informing policy and telling us  predicting what might happen  this was perhaps one of the most um  widely  cited and well recognized papers  that was led by neil ferguson from  imperial college it was an agent-based  simulation um which is a bit of a  different sort of a model it sort of  fits into the seo  framework to some extent but it  explicitly represents every individual  person within the population as an  individual  and explicitly says what they're doing  where they're going who they're  interacting with  and so this model received a lot of  criticism  after it was published because of the  way in which it was coded  and if you look at the outputs from it  it actually gives us  pretty similar outputs to what the scir  framework does it  basically just gives us a bell-shaped  curve so there's a range of  epidemiological models that are all  mechanistic  i guess that's the thing that they share  in common um  but they uh and they're all quite  different in terms of the engine  underneath the bonnet that is driving um  what how the epidemic plays out but  even these very sophisticated or  complicated um  epidemiological models that are based on  agents and individuals interacting still  give us this sort of general bell-shaped  curve which still captures those  fundamental aspects of an unmitigated  epidemic  and so this model was very influential  and its main conclusions i think  was still very sound in spite of the  criticism that it received in that  in that it did alert people to the huge  burden of infection that we might expect  if we did nothing to mitigate the co-19  epidemic  so these these bell-shaped curves tell  part of the story but the thing that  they don't  really tell as well as this  figure that also appeared in the same  paper is just  how enormous the epidemic would have  been have we done nothing from the  outset  because that horizontal red line towards  the bottom of this graph  is the surge critical care capacity so  that's that's additional  resources put in place in the uk or the  us to  allow for additional people to be  admitted to icu on top of what  is normally available and the you can  see just how enormously the  curves smash through that threshold  in the case of a mitigated epidemic but  even in the case of a mitigated epidemic  which is something like the orange curve  where we have  restrictions in place for a certain  period of time in that gray area but  then we release those restrictions and  try  to just get by with case up isolation  and um some milder social distancing  mechan  interventions that is still uh it's a  flatter curve but and  flattening the curve you know i suppose  helps to some extent but it still is  way way way over what um our surge  critical care bed capacity  would be expected to be so um  that was i think very informative very  useful and  was very influential and um that  was useful information to have early in  the epidemic and sort of  was uh incorporated with the fact that  we were observing very high rates of  uh disease and icu occupancy in  countries like italy and we did see  a major catastrophe there in the early  months of the year so  so it became clear that those general  messages from the model were correct and  that the covert 19 epidemic was going to  have a massive effect  um we did we put together a  short paper that's available in med  archive  that does a similar sort of thing to  what was proposed  in that agent-based simulation by the  ferguson group  and imperial college but does just use  an scir framework and so it was  essentially just a compartmental model  and a deterministic model not simulating  individuals  as ferguson's group did so essentially  it's seir  really the we've split a couple of  compartments into two  um for reasons i won't go into but the  the  really the only thing that we wanted to  add on to this was just an  additional compartment just to simulate  people in  intensive care units to explicitly be  able to estimate  the number of people who would be  admitted to icu  over time if we had a major epidemic in  australia  in the top panel here we have a totally  unmitigated epidemic to the left so this  is if  our naught or they are effective or  reproduction number  remains well above two we would see this  huge epidemic that overwhelms critical  capacity with that horizontal dotted  blue line being the  critical capacity if we did  some sort of moderate measures you can  see again that we flattened the curve to  some extent that's the second curve in  the top panel  but the number of people in icu still  massively overwhelms even our surge  critical care  capacity if we try to  knock the reproduction number down even  further to perhaps 1.2  we can keep numbers down to within  critical care bed capacity  but you can see that the epidemic plays  out then for a much  longer period of time and it's perhaps  mid 2022  before the epidemic is over by which  time we might hope to have a vaccine  so so that shows the idea of this is to  illustrate that  the idea of partially mitigating a major  outbreak  in the case of covert 19 was much more  problematic than what  might have been anticipated for an  influenza pandemic just because the  the number of people requiring icu and  the severity of the disease was much  greater for  in the case of covert 19 in the lower  panel there we have what happens if you  just go that little bit further and  right rather than reduce the  reproduction number to 1.2 that you try  to just knock it down somewhere below  one and so if you try to keep it at 0.8  for example  you basically don't have an epidemic the  blue line is then right at the top of  the graph and the number of people  getting infected is right down at the  bottom and so you have very few  uh very limited transmission and you  don't have to worry about critical care  capacity at all  so the message here was that if you're  going to put in place a lot of very  major  interventions to try to knock the  epidemic down from a reproduction number  of 2.4 to 1.2 you might as well  put a couple of extra ones in place and  try to get down to 0.8 and a lot of  countries are trying to do that  such as australia and new zealand  although some countries just don't  have the resources or the opportunity to  be able to do that  the flip side of it though is that we  don't get close to reaching herd  immunity if we take  this pathway so if we think about the  unmitigated epidemic that's the solid  black line  and the green horizontal bar is the herd  immunity threshold  and the vertical axis is the proportion  or the number of people in australia who  are still susceptible to  the epidemic after the first wave so if  we were to have let a huge unmitigated  epidemic occur  as i mentioned we would go past the herd  immunity threshold and more people would  be infected  than is necessary if we get down to that  point of  just carefully trying to find our way to  the herd immunity threshold within our  effective  reproduction number of 1.2 we would  have a very large epidemic as we saw  we'd still overwhelm critical care  capacity  and um and that's the dashed line  if we try to keep things under enough  control to  stay within critical care capacity  that's the dotted line and we're still  nowhere near the herd immunity threshold  whereas if we go through this  intermittent  strategy of trying to keep the epidemic  suppressed and keep the reproduction of  the very  low numbers below one we're not very low  numbers but just somewhere below one  we basically end up with the entire  population remaining fully susceptible  to the epidemic so that means that we  still have the potential  for explosive outbreaks if we release  the brakes on the system and the  reproduction number pops back up above  one so unfortunately this illustrates  that no  strategy is perfect and  any pathway that we choose has a lot of  problems until we have  a vaccine that can um that can provide  us with a  technologically based solution uh  but uh i think it's really important to  understand  things in this context and that the herd  immunity threshold as much as it's  become a bit of a dirty term  is a really important concept for  understanding what's going on in the  background of the epidemic  so why is covert 19 so hard to control  and why is it like this and why haven't  we been able to do what we did with sars  for example in 2003  and control it and why is it also so  different from influenza well i've  already discussed the fact that it's  more severe than influenza  in the case of influenza there were a  series of epidemics of course we tend to  get them every 10 to 20 years  the last really major one was in 1918 so  this graph is from that same document  from the cdc  that plots each of the epidemics  according to their severity and their  transmissibility and infectiousness um  so that if you're in the top right in  that black area you're a  particularly nasty pandemic and if  you're in the bottom left  you're perhaps a bit milder or  more controllable epidemic and you can  see that gradually  over time through the course of the 20th  century and beyond  the pandemic seemed to get a little bit  milder on the whole so the 1918 one was  a disaster but nobody remembers that now  because it's  too long ago for anybody to be able to  tell us about so we just have to read  about it  um and then as the 20th century evolved  and played out the epidemic seemed to  march from that top right down to the  bottom left not consistently and this is  a totally  random thing of course but i think it  just illustrates  how the epidemics and the history of the  pandemics  perhaps load us into a bit of a false  sense of security the ones that we were  able to remember better were the  milder more controllable ones and so we  weren't as prepared as we might be for a  very major  very uh transmissible and very severe  so to understand a little bit about why  19 might be particularly difficult to  control i just want to talk about  the patient's journey through the course  of infection disease  and this is a brief timeline you can  think that at the start of the blue  box there that somebody's been infected  sometimes after that they become  infectious then they might develop  symptoms  they've been detected by uh medical  system and some  time after that they would hopefully be  isolated and  perhaps even later than that they would  they might be notified  to the public health system and their  data might work through the surveillance  system so that we  so they appear in the numbers that we  see every day  um there's some quantities that we talk  about in epidemiological modeling called  the serial interval and the generation  time so if you think about the  person who the index case is that top  line and then the person who they infect  is the bottom line  the generation time is the interval  between one person being infected  getting infected um and the serial  interval is the  time between that first person  developing symptoms and the person they  infected  so this these principles were what  a paper that was published in nature  medicine that looked at data from  a hospital in southern china two  different data sets  this is one of the data sets in which  you can see the exposure window between  two people one who was the index case  who did the infecting and one who was  the secondary case  if you look at the delay between the red  dot and the blue dot  you you can get a sense for when the  primary case had their symptom onset in  the secondary case  got their symptoms and how that relates  to the  window exposure and so that allowed  these  so they they had information basically  on the serial interval  the interval between one person eating  symptoms and their their infected  contact getting symptoms  and they could say when those people  would come into contact  so that gave them enough information to  be able to reconstruct some information  about the infectiousness profiles of  the individual with code the other  piece of information or the other data  set that they had available was what's  called a cycle time which is um  the higher the number the the more virus  somebody is producing i suppose is the  easiest way to think about  it and people had this information  available  from the point of symptom onset onward  and so we could see  that as cycle time went up in number and  that line goes down  the uh as time goes along that quantity  sort of reduces so what we're saying is  that people are getting less infectious  over time from developing symptoms and  the  thing that they really wanted to  emphasize here was that right from the  very start from day zero that  line already appears to be sloping  downwards and the data's a bit noisy but  what they're saying  is that even at the point at which we  start observing people with symptoms  their infectious infectiousness already  seems to be decreasing  and so what that suggests is that the p  came before  their symptoms started and so combined  with that data on the serial interval  we can we can reconstruct some sort of  picture  of how infectiousness plays out relative  and these were some other figures from  this paper and in the top right we have  a sort of a i guess this doesn't come  from data but it's an  illustration of what we think was going  on with sars in 2003 that the primary  case has a period of time  in which they're asymptomatic that's the  blue boxes on the left  and then they develop symptoms as they  enter the purple boxes towards the right  in case of sars we generally thought  that  most of the infectiousness which are the  blue most bell-shaped curves  infectiousness occurred after symptoms  commenced so it meant that public health  interventions had the potential to be  a lot more effective by identifying  people  after they had symptoms and isolating  them promptly you could cut out most of  their transmission  um that's to some extent similar to  seasonal influenza in the bottom right  although there's probably some  transmission that occurs  prior to symptoms starting but these  researchers were able to  present data that suggests that in the  case of covert we might have  something that's a little bit more along  the lines of the panels on the left  so for example in the top panel there we  have the bell-shaped  blue curve having a substantial  proportion of its  mass to the left of symptom onset so a  lot of people are getting  infectious before they have symptoms  which makes it very difficult to  identify these people in time to isolate  them and stop them from  infection so the third thing i'd like to  talk about and probably the last thing  is  is thinking about data and trying to  quantify the covert epidemic which you  would think would be  very easy given how many people have had  um covert and how the internet is making  data very accessible  people and people a lot of people  watching websites like the johns hopkins  website  but if we look at cases or notifications  actually  they don't really tell the whole story  they tell a lot of the story in some  countries like towards the bottom of the  graph here we have that in australia  we've had very high testing capacity  and this is the this is the estimated  proportion of symptomatic cases that we  think  are detected in australia we think most  symptomatic cases  have been detected since pretty early in  the epidemic  and that's been very variable but but a  lot of countries have done well  uh such as malaysia and australia in  but in contrast in the top part of the  panel there are a lot of countries that  particularly for ambulatory patients who  have not admitted to hospital there just  was very very little testing capacity  and  and in a sense for some of these  countries testing was not really a major  component of their response so for  example in the uk and france the  proportion  of um people who were not admitted to  hospital who  received the test and were effectively  diagnosed was very very small  which has implications for whether those  people couldn't be isolated of course  um and so it has implications from the  perspective of the public health  response but it also  has implications in terms of  understanding the epidemic in these  countries  so for example in france we really  have to use modeling to give us a sense  for how big the epidemic  is and those models um  this is an example of a model that was  produced by simon koshmas group  published in science which was a very  major and influential  piece that aimed to give a sense of the  epidemic in france it  did as good a job as it could it was  fitted to  the data that were available that  included things like hospital admissions  and general ward beds  um being used and um and made some use  of notification data  but there were a fair few assumptions  that needed to be fed into this model  such as the infection fatality rate  which i'll talk about a little bit later  um and so i guess what the model in the  sense is doing here is that it's  compensating for the fact that we don't  really have a good sense from  surveillance data about what the size of  the epidemic was in france  so the so the model was needed to  reconstruct that  and there's things like the proportion  of people infected  using surveillance data because such a  small proportion of people were actually  identified  is not a satisfactory way to work that  out and so the model  can give us a sense for that cro surveys  are an even better way probably of  working that out  um but for example in panel e you can  see that they estimated that even  after this major um major epidemic  only around four percent or or  six to seven at most of the population  had been infected  so so some of the most important  quantities think about uh  or yeah other infection fatality rate  the case fatality rate is something  that's discussed a lot and it's  important to understand the difference  between these two things so the case  fatality rate is the  number of deaths divided by the number  of cases  that are identified so so if you look at  the  daily notifications for some of these  countries that's the denominator in the  case of the case fatality rate  but as we've just discussed it's  extremely dependent upon  the um the testing capacity  because in some countries they're just  not the denominator is really very  unknown  um or might be very small compared to  the number of infections that have  occurred  and so that inflates the case fatality  rate so what we're really more  interested in as epidemiological  modelers or epidemiologists is the  infection fatality  rate which we think is a little bit more  consistent between countries regardless  of testing capacity  and that's defined defined as the  proportion  of infections that result in deaths so  the number of coca-19 deaths divided by  the number of infections  and that's a a quantity that we're more  interested in but then it's also  a little bit more difficult to identify  because we don't necessarily know how  many infections  occurred in a population so even models  like  the one that i presented from single  question  have limitations and they have to make  assumptions about things like the  infection  so the infection fatality rate in papers  like this and other ones  are estimated from small groups of  patients who  are very well described so an example is  the diamond princess which is a cruise  ship  that people probably have heard of that  docked in  japan and had a major outbreak and just  about everybody on the cruise ship  was tested and so we know  size of that outbreak pretty well and it  was a  it was an outbreak that had your  particular characteristics of its own  um which i won't go into but it's  assumed that we can  estimate the infection fatality rate  because we  because in this case it was possible to  identify all of the infections  so we think there were 712 infections on  the diamond princess  and we um and we have some sense of the  number of deaths that occurred although  even for that there are still some uh  people who are in  long-term icu who um uh  well at the time of publication the  kosher movie paper they were but  even even determining the number of  deaths there are some issues but we can  have a pretty good idea  so there were 14 deaths and and four  people admitted to long-term icu so  somewhere between 14 and 18  deaths among 712 people  and though they were distributed very um  very much towards the older age groups  and so  papers like this or studies like this  have been what has been used to estimate  the infection fatality rate which we  know is of course very very different  according to age group  um but i think so the reason that i've  described this is because it just  highlights how  in spite of the number of deaths and the  number of cases that have occurred  to work out the infection fatality rate  we're still going back to  relatively small groups of people who  have been well described to  calculate these critical quantities for  our models  and perhaps the only other way to think  about  um the impact of coca-19 is to look at  all-cause mortality which is  a really useful thing to do and we can  see that it's spiked up in just about  all countries across the world since the  start of the pandemic  um to a very major extent and  um so you can see the ones that are  identified  as having been covered 19 deaths here  illustrated in orange  and that sits on top of a sort of a gray  background underneath  that gives more of a sense of the  all-cause mortality  and for the particular time of the year  the dashed line is what you would expect  for each of these countries  and so you can see again how the how the  infection the number of deaths  mortality or cause mortality rate has  really risen up very markedly above that  um and so in a lot of cases that's to a  greater extent than the orange  area would it would suggest so that even  some deaths that are not attributable  not attributable or haven't been  attributed to copen 19  are probably a consequence  that's something that's really um  important and good to do  in countries that have good vital  registration systems and good  records of deaths but actually that's  not  always the case and a surprising number  of countries around around the world and  a lot of low and middle income countries  do not have  high quality vital registration systems  so getting a sense of what's going on in  low and middle income countries is  difficult  um even in countries that have  some data such as jakarta where there's  monthly information  on variables we do still see a similar  pattern so it does seem that  um the extent of the pandemic in lower  middle income countries  is probably greater than was previously  the  well probably greater than is recognized  from surveillance statistics  so in this example in jakarta you can  see that in march and april of this year  there were considerably more  deaths overalls considerably more  burials occurring even though a very  small portion of that additional number  was attributed  to cobra 19. so we think that there was  probably quite substantial transmission  going on under the surface  so i will finish up there but just  quickly to recap and i've left a few  slides out at the end  um but happy to discuss other issues in  the  discussion forum later over 19 has  presented huge new challenges for our  models  even constructing models that capture  the epidemic by the time  you've published them or even  constructed them a month or a few weeks  later  you might be in the middle of the second  wave and you have to really really  totally rethink about what your  model needs to look like modeling has  been undertaken at breathtaking  breathtaking speed it has given some  really important  messages that have influenced policy in  the early phases of the epidemic and  many of them  inspired the critic despite the  criticism were broadly correct  and even the simplest models can provide  insights into the current situation and  how we should be responding to it  and despite the number of cases reported  the data that we're using to inform our  models remains imperfect  thanks so much for your time  thank you associate professor james  schroer for a great presentation

